        <section>
          <h2>The lessons so far</h2>
          
          <div class="cards">
            <article class="card">
              <h3>Understanding HTML</h3>
              <p>
                Semantic elements are critical for a modern web page. A
                <code>&lt;header&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;nav&gt;</code>,
                <code>&lt;main&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;footer&gt;</code> tell
                browsers and screen readers what each region <em>means</em>,
                not just how it looks.
              </p>
              <a href="index2.html">Read the selectors lesson →</a>
            </article>

            <article class="card">
              <h3>Understanding CSS</h3>
              <p>
                CSS is the styling language for the web. Selectors decide
                <em>what</em> gets styled, the cascade decides <em>which</em>
                rule wins, and the box model decides how much room every element
                takes up.
              </p>
              <a href="index2.html">Try the live demo →</a>
            </article>

            <article class="card">
              <h3>Flexbox</h3>
              <p>
                One direction at a time. Flexbox distributes space along a
                single axis, which makes it the right tool for navigation bars,
                button rows and toolbars.
              </p>
              <a href="flex.html">Open the flex demo →</a>
            </article>

            <article class="card">
              <h3>Grid</h3>
              <p>
                Two directions at once. Grid places items into named rows and
                columns the reason this page has a sidebar, a topbar and three
                side panels that all hold their position.
              </p>
              <a href="grid.html">Open the grid demo →</a>
            </article>

            <article class="card">
              <h3>Forms</h3>
              <p>
                Inputs, labels, selects, radio groups and a textarea, wired up
                with proper <code>for</code> and <code>id</code> pairs so every
                label actually belongs to its field.
              </p>
              <a href="form.html">Fill in the form →</a>
            </article>

            <article class="card">
              <h3>Responsiveness</h3>
              <p>
                The layout reflows three times: four columns on a desktop, three
                on a laptop, and a single stacked column on a phone with the
                sidebar turned into a scrollable strip of pills.
              </p>
              <a href="grid.html">See how it reflows →</a>
            </article>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2>How this page is put together</h2>
          <p>
            The whole site is one CSS grid with six named areas. Each page fills
            the same slots with different content, so navigating never changes
            the furniture around you.
          </p>
          <pre><code>.app {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-areas:
    "side  nav   nav   nav"
    "side  main  main  hist"
    "side  prof  prof  user"
    "side  foot  foot  foot";
}</code></pre>
          <div class="note">
            <strong>Try it:</strong> drag the edge of your browser window
            narrower. At 1180px the history panel slides under the main column,
            and at 860px everything becomes a single column.
          </div>
        </section>
